## 🐛 Bug

It seems a C++ exception is not caught and converted to a Python Exception which makes e.g. test_anomaly_detect_nan fail with:

```
Running test_autograd ... [2021-05-05 13:10:00.082901]
Executing ['/sw/installed/Python/3.8.6-GCCcore-10.2.0/bin/python', 'test_autograd.py', '-v'] ... [2021-05-05 13:10:00.083026]
test_accumulate_grad (__main__.TestAutograd) ... ok
test_accumulate_grad_tensor_reference (__main__.TestAutograd) ... ok
test_anomaly_assign_parent_cleanup (__main__.TestAutograd) ... test_autograd.py:3707: UserWarning: Anomaly Detection has been enabled. This mode will increase the runtime and should only be enabled for debugging.
  with detect_anomaly():
ok
test_anomaly_detect_nan (__main__.TestAutograd) ... terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::runtime_error'
  what():  Function 'MyFuncBackward' returned nan values in its 0th output.
Traceback (most recent call last):
  File "run_test.py", line 926, in <module>
    main()
  File "run_test.py", line 905, in main
    raise RuntimeError(err_message)
RuntimeError: test_autograd failed! Received signal: SIGIOT
 (at easybuild/tools/run.py:564 in parse_cmd_output)
```

## To Reproduce

Steps to reproduce the behavior:

1. Compile with kineto support & run tests 

## Environment

 - PyTorch Version (e.g., 1.0): 1.8.1
 - OS (e.g., Linux): RHEL
 - How you installed PyTorch (`conda`, `pip`, source): source
 - Python version: 3.8.6
 - CUDA/cuDNN version: 11.1.1
 - GPU models and configuration: 8x A100
 - Any other relevant information: GCC 10.2.0

## Additional context

After finding this I dug (very) deep and noticed, that C++ exceptions in general don't work anymore. E.g. I added the following code to the top of torch/csrc/autograd/engine.cpp which will be executed very early:

```
int make_foo(){
    try{
        throw std::runtime_error("foo");
    }catch(...){}
    return 42;
}
static int foo = make_foo();
```

And after a mere `python -c 'import torch'` the app crashes with the same `terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::runtime_error'`. This fully reproduces every time with the self-compiled version, but not with the PyPi version. However that is not built with CUDA 11 and hence does not support our A100 GPUs

Using `git bisect` I found https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/commit/e34992ebee to be the first failing commit. Checking this it seems that disabling Kineto in CMake avoids the issue.

However I'm very puzzled how merely using Kineto can completely break the C++ exception handling.

Reducing this further I got this minimal example:

```
#include <stdexcept>
#include <cupti.h>
void foo(){
  CUpti_SubscriberHandle subscriber;
  cuptiSubscribe(&subscriber, (CUpti_CallbackFunc) nullptr, nullptr);
}
int main(){
  try{
    throw std::runtime_error("Hello");
  }catch(...){
  }
}
```

Compiled with `g++ testRaise.cpp -l/sw/installed/CUDAcore/11.1.1/extras/CUPTI/lib64/libcupti_static.a -ldl -lpthread -lrt` and running makes it crash using a recentish g++. With the system GCC 4.8 it works.

--> I think linking the static CUPTI is not a good idea in general. Using the shared lib works.

